\description{
Data from a study investigating how prediction of body location influences 
feelings of ownership and disownership using virtual reality body illusions.
Participants experienced two illusion types (virtual Rubber Hand Illusion 
and First-Person Perspective Full Body Illusion) with their body aligned or 
misaligned with a virtual avatar.
}
\details{
This dataset contains proprioceptive drift measurements from a within-subjects 
experiment with 80 participants. The study used a 2×2×2 factorial design:
\itemize{
  \item Illusion type (RHI vs FBI)
  \item Location (Colocation vs Dislocation)  
  \item Time (T0 vs T1)
}

Each participant completed all 8 conditions (2×2×2) with 10 pointing trials 
per condition, resulting in 80 trials per participant.

The study manipulated whether a virtual body (hand or full body) was spatially 
aligned or misaligned with the participant's real body. After visual exposure 
(T0) and again after synchronous visuo-tactile stimulation (T1), participants 
pointed to where they perceived their left hand (RHI) or leg (FBI) to be 
located in space.

Key findings:
\itemize{
  \item Both illusions increased feelings of ownership toward virtual body
  \item Proprioceptive drift occurred toward virtual body in misaligned 
    conditions after illusion (T1)
  \item No drift in aligned conditions
  \item Disownership feelings emerged only in misaligned FBI condition
}

This data is particularly useful for teaching mixed-effects models with 
random intercepts, as measurements are nested within participants who 
experienced multiple conditions.
}
